## The Bug

The goroutines capture the loop variable `i` **by reference** (it's a closure). By the time any goroutine actually runs, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to `5`. All five goroutines therefore print the same final value: `5`.

This is a well-known pitfall with Go's loop variable semantics in versions prior to 1.22 (where each iteration got its own variable).

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine function so each one captures its own copy:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

Each goroutine now receives its own parameter `i` with the value from that specific iteration, so you'll get `0 1 2 3 4` (in some order, since goroutine scheduling is nondeterministic).

