## Bug Identification

The crash is on **line 2**:

```javascript
const city = order.shipping_address.city;
```

When `order.shipping_address` is `undefined`, accessing `.city` on it throws exactly the error you're seeing: `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')`.

**Why it works locally but not in production:** Your local test orders likely always have a `shipping_address` populated. In production, some orders won't — think digital goods, in-store pickups, or orders created via a different code path that omits the address.

## Fix

Use optional chaining to safely handle the missing `shipping_address`:

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function getShippingZone(order) {
    const city = order.shipping_address?.city;
    const zones = {
        "New York": "east",
        "Los Angeles": "west",
        "Chicago": "central",
    };
    return zones[city] || "standard";
}
</solution>

If `shipping_address` is `undefined`, `city` will be `undefined`, and `zones[undefined]` returns `undefined`, so the function safely falls through to `"standard"` — which is the same behavior you already intended with the `|| "standard"` fallback.

